{{also|Appendix:Variations of "soc"}}
==English==

===Etymology 1===
From ''[[sociology]]''.

====Pronunciation====
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/səʊʃ/}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-soc (sh).wav|Audio (UK)}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/soʊʃ/}}
* {{rhymes|en|əʊʃ|s=1}}
* {{homophones|en|sosh}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|slang|uncountable}} [[sociology|Sociology]] or [[social science]].
# {{lb|en|slang|countable}} [[upper class|Upper class]] [[youth]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1967|author={{w|S. E. Hinton}}|title={{w|The Outsiders (novel)|The Outsiders}}|page=2|passage=We get jumped by the '''Socs'''. I'm not sure how you spell it, but it's the abbreviation for the Socials, the jet set, the West-side rich kids.}}

=====Alternative forms=====
* {{l|en|Soc}}

===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|soke}}, {{m|enm|sok}}, {{m|enm|soc}}, from {{inh|en|ang|sōcn}}. More at {{m|en|soke}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/sɒk/}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/sɑk/}}
* {{audio|en|En-au-soc.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun|-}}

# {{lb|en|UK|legal|historical}} The [[lord]]'s power or [[privilege]] of holding a [[court]] in a [[district]], as in [[manor]] or [[lordship]]; [[jurisdiction]] of [[cause]]s, and the limits of that jurisdiction.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1869| title=Commentaries on the History, Constitution, and Chartered Franchises of the City of London|author=George Norton | page=96| passage=As proprietors of the '''soc''', the lords claimed a great number of fees and perquisites, payable by all classes of people, whether free or servile, who negotiated any affairs within the soc, and which no doubt formed in themselves a considerable source of revenue.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2001| title=A Concise History of the Common Law |author=Theodore Frank Thomas Plucknett | page=96| passage=Doubts have recently been cast upon Maitland's view that the Anglo-Saxon "sac and '''soc'''" included the right to hold a petty court, to compel tenants to attend it, and to take profits from it.}}
#: {{syn|en|soke}}
# {{lb|en|UK|obsolete}} An exclusive privilege formerly claimed by [[miller]]s of [[grind]]ing all the [[corn]] used within the manor or township in which the [[mill]] stands.

=====Derived terms=====
* {{l|en|soc and sac}}

